#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wad — DUE vs LATENT env of a load image set, by getenv-shaped *use*.

Ancestor xref made DUE a getenv/env::var *call-site* proof. lash added
one-hop wrappers (`mov x0, x3; bl getenv`, `add x0, x1, #4`). The leftover
named in both CANDIDATEs: inlined Rust `env::var` (kizu) builds a stack
`CString` then `getenv` — packed literals never sit in x0 at the `bl`.

wad keeps the two columns, keeps one-hop wrappers, and recovers that
CString hop. A function that memcpy's (ptr, len) onto the stack and feeds
the buffer to getenv (or a getenv wrapper) is env::var-shaped. Callers
that pass a packed `&str` become DUE. Stack-materialized names at a
getenv site (immediate ASCII, rodata copy) are DUE too. Orphans stay
not-DUE. rustc's 12k LLVM opcodes stay out. `--min-evidence call` stays
a real filter.

  DUE     names a getenv-shaped site *uses* (direct, one wrapper, or CString)
  LATENT  names the image *did* document
  BOTH    intersection

DYLD/`LD_PRELOAD` `--trace` is not the second column (malloc reentry + SIP).
This is not strings(1).
"""

